<markdown_cell>
# Comparing pipeline exit-code handling in Bash: `set -o pipefail` vs `PIPESTATUS` vs shell-context substitution

Reference written against Bash 5.3.15 (patchlevel 15, bash53-015, 2026-06-09; 5.3 released 2025-07-05). [source: https://mirrors.ibiblio.org/gnu/bash/bash-5.3-patches/]

A Bash pipeline reports the exit status of the **last** stage by default. Three distinct mechanisms recover the status of earlier or specific stages. This notebook compares them and shows when each is the right tool.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## The problem: a failing stage hides behind a succeeding one

A pipeline such as `npm run build | tee build.log` reports the status of `tee`, which almost always exits 0. A failed compile therefore ships a green checkmark. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# Build "fails", tee "succeeds" — the pipeline reports tee's status (0).
( exit 1 ) | cat
echo "pipeline exit=$?"   # 0 — the failure is invisible
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Option 1 — `set -o pipefail`

`pipefail` changes the pipeline's reported status to the rightmost **non-zero** exit code among all stages. It is the standard hardening fix for masked failures. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]

Note: `set -e` alone does not fire inside `cmd | other`, so a failing pipeline stage still yields a zero status — `pipefail` must be paired with it.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
set -o pipefail

# Without pipefail this pipeline reports 0; with pipefail it reports 1.
( exit 1 ) | cat
echo "pipeline exit=$?"   # 1 — pipefail surfaces the failure
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Option 2 — `PIPESTATUS` array

`PIPESTATUS` is an array holding the exit code of each stage of the most recent pipeline. Unlike `pipefail`, it reveals **every** stage's status, not just the worst one. Caveat: it is overwritten by the next command, so copy it immediately. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
true | ( exit 2 ) | true
echo "pipeline exit=$?"                 # 0
echo "stage codes: ${PIPESTATUS[@]}"    # 0 2 0

# Capture before the next command clobbers it:
true | ( exit 2 ) | true
statuses=("${PIPESTATUS[@]}")
echo "captured: ${statuses[*]}"         # 0 2 0
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Common pitfall — reading `PIPESTATUS` too late

Any command between the pipeline and the read overwrites `PIPESTATUS`. A stray `echo` for debugging will erase it.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
true | ( exit 2 ) | true
echo "debug"                            # this line resets PIPESTATUS
echo "${PIPESTATUS[@]}"                 # 0 0 0 — the real codes are gone
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Option 3 — Bash 5.3 shell-context command substitution `${ command; }`

Bash 5.3 introduced `${ command; }` (and `${|command;}`), which execute **in the current shell context** instead of forking a subshell. The headline effect for exit-code work: the command's own status is available directly as `$?` after the assignment, with no subshell to mask it. This is useful when you want a value **and** a trustworthy status from one call. [source: https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bash-announce/2025-07/msg00000.html]
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
# Classic $(...) forks a subshell; the assignment status wins, hiding the inner exit.
result=$( exit 3 ); echo "after \$(...): $?"   # 0

# Bash 5.3 ${ command; } runs in the current shell context; the status is the command's own.
result=${ exit 3; }; echo "after \${ command; }: $?"   # 3
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## `${| command; }` form — result into `REPLY`

The `${|command;}` variant runs in the current shell and leaves the output in `REPLY` (Bash 5.3). [source: https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bash-announce/2025-07/msg00000.html]
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
${| printf '%s' "hello"; }
echo "REPLY=$REPLY"   # hello
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Comparison

| Mechanism | What it gives you | Granularity | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| `set -o pipefail` | Worst (rightmost non-zero) stage status as the pipeline status | Whole pipeline (single code) | Default hardening; a CI step fails when any stage fails |
| `PIPESTATUS` | Per-stage exit codes of the last pipeline | Per stage | You need to know exactly which stage failed, or branch on individual stages |
| `${ command; }` (Bash 5.3) | Command output **and** its own exit status, no subshell fork | Single command | Capture a value and its status in one call; avoid subshell masking |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## When to reach for each

- Default to `set -o pipefail` in every non-trivial script — it is the cheapest way to stop a green pipeline from hiding a red stage. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]
- Reach for `PIPESTATUS` when the *identity* of the failing stage matters — for example, a multi-stage data pipeline where stage 2 (transform) failing is a different incident from stage 3 (publish) failing.
- Reach for `${ command; }` (Bash 5.3) when you must capture output **and** trust the exit code of the producing command in one expression, without the subshell fork that `$(...)` introduces.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Verify

1. `set -o pipefail` path: run `( exit 1 ) | cat` with and without `pipefail`; confirm the reported code flips from 0 to 1.
2. `PIPESTATUS` path: run `true | ( exit 2 ) | true` and print `${PIPESTATUS[@]}` on the very next line; confirm `0 2 0`.
3. Shell-context substitution path (Bash 5.3+): run `result=${ exit 3; }; echo $?` and confirm `3`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Common errors

- **Reading `PIPESTATUS` after another command:** the next simple command (even `echo`) clobbers it — assign `"${PIPESTATUS[@]}"` to a local array on the immediate next line.
- **`set -e` without `pipefail`:** a failing pipeline stage still produces a zero status. Pair them. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]
- **`local var=$(cmd)` masks status:** assigning inside a `local` declaration swallows `$?`; assign on a separate line if you need the code. [source: https://www.mechanicalrock.io/blog/modern-bash]
- **Bash 3.2 on macOS:** `${ command; }` requires 5.3. `/bin/bash` on macOS is 3.2 — use `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and a newer install. [source: https://mirrors.ibiblio.org/gnu/bash/bash-5.3-patches/]
</markdown_cell>

